+</aside>
+            <h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion: Innovation as a Science of Learning</h2><p>By treating innovation as a <em>science of learning</em> rather than a one-time launch, Product Thinking allows businesses to build "painkillers" — solutions to critical problems — rather than "vitamins" — nice-to-haves that disappear from the budget during the next cost review.</p><section class="takeaways" id="takeaways"><h2>The five commitments of Product Thinking</h2><p class="meta">🧠</p><ol class="tk-list tk-list--num"><li><strong>Treat products as living systems:</strong> They never reach "done." They evolve with customer expectations and competitive landscapes.</li><li><strong>Balance all four risks:</strong> Desirability, Feasibility, Viability, and Usability must all be addressed — ignoring any one produces Builder's Trap failures.</li><li><strong>Fund outcomes, not features:</strong> Cross-functional teams empowered to deliver long-term outcomes consistently outperform project teams funded for fixed scopes.</li><li><strong>Respect the PMF layers:</strong> No layer can be skipped. Problem-Market Fit → Solution-Market Fit → Product-Market Fit. Each is a gate, not a milestone.</li><li><strong>Measure learning, not just output:</strong> The ultimate competitive advantage in 2026 is not how fast you build — it is how fast you learn, iterate, and adapt.</li></ol></section>
+            <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p class="meta">Common questions on Product Thinking, PLG, and organizational change.</p><div class="faq faq--prose">
+      <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span>What is the difference between Product Thinking and Agile development?</span><span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></button><div class="faq__a"><div><p>Agile is a delivery framework — it governs how teams work in sprints and iterate on builds. Product Thinking is a strategic mindset — it governs <strong>what</strong> gets built and why. Product Thinking answers the question "What should we build?" before Agile answers "How do we build it?" Both are necessary; neither replaces the other.</p></div></div></div>
+      <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span>How is Product-Led Growth different from traditional Sales-Led Growth?</span><span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></button><div class="faq__a"><div><p>In Sales-Led Growth, the sales team is the primary acquisition vehicle — expensive, doesn't scale linearly. In Product-Led Growth, the product itself drives acquisition by delivering value before users pay. <strong>The product is the salesperson.</strong> In 2026, the most competitive SaaS companies run hybrid models: PLG to land users efficiently, Sales-Led to expand into enterprise contracts.</p></div></div></div>
+      <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span>What is Conway&#x27;s Law and why does it matter for product teams?</span><span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></button><div class="faq__a"><div><p>Conway's Law states that organizations design systems that mirror their own communication structures. If your org chart has silos between engineering, design, and product, <strong>your software will have those same silos</strong> — disconnected modules that don't talk to each other. Cross-functional Product Mode teams break this pattern by building communication into the team structure before building it into the software.</p></div></div></div>
+      <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span>How do you measure Product-Market Fit quantitatively?</span><span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></button><div class="faq__a"><div><p>Two complementary signals: (1) the <strong>Sean Ellis 40% Test</strong> — if 40%+ of surveyed active users say they'd be "very disappointed" without the product, you have a baseline. (2) <strong>Cohort retention curves</strong> — if curves flatten at a meaningful level (rather than declining toward zero), a subset of users has made the product a habit. Both signals together, not either alone.</p></div></div></div>
+      <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span>Can Product Thinking be applied to existing products, not just new ones?</span><span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></button><div class="faq__a"><div><p>Yes — and it is often even more valuable for existing products that have accumulated feature debt and unclear positioning. The four-risk framework (Desirability, Feasibility, Viability, Usability) applied to an existing product acts as a diagnostic: it identifies which risks have been neglected and which features exist for historical rather than strategic reasons. The PMF layer framework is equally applicable to re-validating an existing product's fit as it is to discovering fit for a new one.</p></div></div></div></div>
