+            <section class="takeaways" id="takeaways"><h2>Key Takeaways for Decision Makers</h2><p class="meta">What separates successful SME transformations</p><ul class="tk-list"><li><strong>Start with the problem, not the tool:</strong> Diagnose process gaps before buying any subscriptions. Mapping your workflows costs nothing — buying the wrong tool costs months.</li><li><strong>Measure outcomes, not activity:</strong> Track "Time to Respond to a Lead" or "Revenue per Employee" — not "Number of Tools Implemented."</li><li><strong>Transformation is a mindset, not a project:</strong> It has no end date. It is a permanent mode of operation that requires continuous improvement, not a one-time implementation.</li><li><strong>Prioritize cybersecurity from day one:</strong> SMEs are prime targets because they often lack specialized security departments. Digital resilience is a prerequisite — not an afterthought.</li><li><strong>The gap is widening every quarter:</strong> The roadmap is clear and the technology is accessible. The only strategy that will definitely not work is waiting.</li></ul></section>
+            <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p class="meta">Common questions on SME digital transformation, costs, and strategy.</p><div class="faq faq--prose">
+      <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span>What is the difference between digitization, digitalization, and digital transformation?</span><span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></button><div class="faq__a"><div><p><strong>Digitization</strong> is converting analog data to digital (e.g., scanning a paper invoice). <strong>Digitalization</strong> is using digital tools to improve a process (e.g., using Slack for communication). <strong>Digital transformation</strong> is reimagining your entire business model and culture through technology — so the process itself is fundamentally different, not just faster.</p></div></div></div>
+      <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span>Is digital transformation too expensive for a small business?</span><span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></button><div class="faq__a"><div><p>No. Cloud computing and SaaS have removed the need for massive upfront capital investments. Transformation can be phased into low-cost sprints that produce "Quick Wins" in months 1–6 — and the ROI from those quick wins funds the next phase. The 18-month roadmap is designed specifically to be self-funding at each stage.</p></div></div></div>
+      <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span>Why do 62% of digital transformations in SMEs fail?</span><span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></button><div class="faq__a"><div><p>The most common failure pattern is buying technology before diagnosing the business problem. Businesses invest in tools without mapping their workflows or identifying which processes are actually worth automating. <strong>A chaotic process doesn't become an efficient one by digitizing it — it becomes a chaotically fast, chaotic process.</strong></p></div></div></div>
+      <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span>How does &quot;Low-Code&quot; help SMEs with digital transformation?</span><span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></button><div class="faq__a"><div><p>Low-code platforms allow non-technical users to build purpose-built applications using drag-and-drop interfaces. This helps SMEs bridge the technical expertise gap and build custom solutions without hiring an entire engineering team — particularly useful for process automation in Sprint 1 and customer experience improvements in Sprint 2.</p></div></div></div>
+      <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span>When should an SME use AI in its transformation roadmap?</span><span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></button><div class="faq__a"><div><p><strong>Only after Sprints 1 and 2 are complete.</strong> AI applied to manual, messy operations produces chaotically fast, chaotic results. AI applied to automated workflows on top of clean, connected data is transformative. Sprint 3 (months 13–18) is specifically designed to layer AI after the data and process foundations are solid.</p></div></div></div></div>
